#!/usr/bin/env python3
# burrow-gen: the code burrow generates or checks rather than writes by hand.
# The subcommands have little in common except that none of them belongs in a
# compiler.
#
#     burrow-gen reflect      type descriptors for structs you already have
#     burrow-gen amalgamate   the whole library as one burrow.c and one burrow.h
#     burrow-gen doc          the code in docs/ checked against real programs
#     burrow-gen collisions   burrow's link names against other libraries' symbols
#
# REFLECT
#
# The X macro DSL in burrow/declare.h is the default way to get a descriptor,
# and it needs no tool and no build step. It also needs you to write your struct
# as a field list, which is fine for new code and is not going to happen to a
# hundred thousand lines of existing C. This reads the structs you already have
# and emits the same descriptors the DSL would have.
#
#     /* burrow:reflect */
#     typedef struct {
#         Int X     BURROW_TAG("json:\"x\"");
#         Int Y     BURROW_TAG("json:\"y\"");
#         Str Label BURROW_TAG("json:\"label,omitempty\"");
#     } Point;
#
#     burrow-gen reflect shapes.h -o shapes_gen.c -Iinclude
#
# Neither path is deprecated and neither is preferred. The DSL is the default
# because of the no build step promise, and this exists because a large existing
# codebase will not restructure its headers to get %v to work.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COMPUTE
#
# It does not compute offsets, sizes or alignments. It emits offsetof, sizeof
# and _Alignof and lets the compiler that is actually building for the target
# work them out. That is not laziness, it is the only correct answer: a
# generator that computed 12 for an offset would be computing it for whatever
# machine the generator ran on, and the whole point of a portable library is
# that the answer is different on some of them. The generator's job is to find
# out which fields exist and what they are called, and nothing else.
#
# WHAT IT NEEDS
#
# Python 3 and a libclang shared library, which you already have if you compile
# C with clang. There is no pip install: the binding is about a hundred lines of
# ctypes at the bottom of this file, because a code generator that makes you
# build a virtualenv first is a code generator people work around.
#
# libclang is not needed to build burrow, to use the DSL, or to compile what
# this emits. It is needed to run this, once, when your structs change.
#
# AMALGAMATE
#
#     burrow-gen amalgamate --out dist --source-id "$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
#
# writes dist/burrow.c, dist/burrow.h and dist/burrow-manifest.json. The two C
# files are the whole library, the way SQLite ships: add burrow.c to a build,
# include burrow.h, and there is nothing else to install. burrow.h is the public
# headers in dependency order. burrow.c is every private header and then every
# source file, each behind a marker and a #line so that errors and debuggers
# name the file the code really lives in.
#
# It is generated and never edited, and it is not clever. Nothing is rewritten
# except include lines, which become comments so the line numbers stay right. A
# source file's own macros are undefined at the end of that file, because two
# files are allowed to mean different things by STACK_FLOOR. What it cannot fix
# is two files with the same static name, and tools/check-statics.sh is what
# keeps those out of the tree. Running it twice on the same tree gives the same
# bytes, and CI checks that. It needs Python 3 and nothing else.
#
#     burrow-gen amalgamate --packages context,io --out dist
#
# keeps only those packages and what they need. Every package that can be left
# out is also wrapped in a BURROW_OMIT_ macro, so the full file can be trimmed
# from the compiler command line instead. The PACKAGES notes further down say
# how the generator decides what belongs where.
#
# docs/design/15-build-deploy.md section 2 is the design, including the options
# that are not here yet: choosing targets, a prefix, and splitting the file.
#
# DOC
#
#     burrow-gen doc --check          every marked block matches its program
#     burrow-gen doc --sync           rewrite the marked blocks from the programs
#     burrow-gen doc --check --run    and compile and run every program too
#
# A C block in the docs is not the source of truth for anything. The program it
# came from is, a whole file under docs/examples that compiles and runs, and the
# block is a copy of it or of a named part of it. The markdown says which with
# a comment on the line before the fence, which GitHub does not show:
#
#     <!-- example: ../examples/synctest/wait.c#body -->
#
# The path is relative to the markdown file, and #body names the lines between
# "// doc: body" and "// doc: end" in the program, dedented. Without a name the
# block is the whole program, minus any doc markers. A block that is only there
# to show something that does not compile, like the mistake a guide is warning
# about, says so instead:
#
#     <!-- not compiled: the wrong way, for contrast -->
#
# --check fails when a block and its program disagree, and --sync is the fix.
# For the markdown files named in docs/examples/checked.txt it also fails on a
# C block with neither comment, so a guide on that list stays converted.
#
# --run builds the amalgamation into a temporary directory, compiles every
# program under docs/examples against it with -Wall -Wextra -Werror, and runs
# each one. A program that ends with a comment of the form
#
#     /* Output:
#     what it prints
#     */
#
# has to print exactly that. The programs include burrow/<name>.h the way a
# program using the split headers does, and each of those names is a one line
# header that includes the amalgamated burrow.h, so both spellings are tested.
#
# COLLISIONS
#
#     burrow-gen collisions /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libz.so.1 ...
#
# burrow's public names have no prefix, which is the right call for the people
# writing code against it and a risk for the people linking it: a program that
# links burrow and some other library that also defines str_eq, or crc32_update,
# gets a duplicate symbol from a static link and, worse, a silent winner from a
# dynamic one. This reads the symbols every library named on the command line
# exports and fails on any that burrow defines too.
#
# burrow's side is two sets. One is what the built library defines today, read
# out of build/libburrow.a with nm. The other is every name burrow will define
# once all of Go is ported: tools/burrow-coverage already turns each func,
# method and var in Go's API manifest into the C name the naming rules give it,
# so a collision with a package nobody has written yet shows up now, while the
# name can still be changed for free. Constants and types are not link names and
# are not checked.
#
# The other side can be an ELF shared object, a static archive, a Mach-O dylib,
# an Apple SDK .tbd stub or a Windows import library, and a directory stands
# for every one of those in it. A collision somebody has looked at and decided
# is harmless goes in tools/collision-waivers.txt with the reason.
# tools/check-collisions.sh is the list of libraries make check and CI use.
